    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 4 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 4.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 9 (Serving District 4)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>333 Seventh Avenue, New York, NY 10001</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:917-339-1600">917-339-1600</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em></li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal).</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-bullhorn"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 4
    </h2>

    <div class="advocacy-content" style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
        <p>Navigating the special education system in NYC District 4 can be challenging. Here are some tips to help you advocate effectively for your child:</p>

        <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
            <li><strong>Know Your Rights:</strong> Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Part 200 Regulations. IDEA guarantees your child the right to a Free and Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) in the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE). Part 200 outlines the specific procedures and requirements for special education in NYS. <a href="http://www.p12.nysed.gov/specialed/lawsregs/part200/home.html" target="_blank">Review Part 200 here.</a></li>
            <li><strong>Document Everything:</strong> Keep detailed records of all communication with the school, including emails, letters, and phone calls. This documentation can be crucial if you need to file a complaint or request a hearing.</li>
            <li><strong>Request Evaluations in Writing:</strong> If you believe your child needs special education services, make a formal, written request for an evaluation to the CSE. According to Part 200.4(a)(1), the district has 60 school days from the date they receive your written consent to complete the evaluation and hold a CSE meeting.</li>
            <li><strong>Prepare for CSE Meetings:</strong> Before each CSE meeting, review your child's IEP (Individualized Education Program) and any relevant evaluation reports. Make a list of your concerns and goals for your child's education. Bring copies of any documents you want the CSE to consider.</li>
            <li><strong>Bring an Advocate:</strong> You have the right to bring an advocate or attorney to CSE meetings. An advocate can help you understand your rights and advocate for your child's needs. Consider contacting a non-profit organization that provides free or low-cost advocacy services.</li>
            <li><strong>Understand the IEP Process:</strong> The IEP is a legally binding document that outlines your child's educational goals, services, and accommodations. Ensure that the IEP is tailored to your child's individual needs and that you understand all of its components. Per IDEA, you have the right to participate in all IEP meetings and to disagree with the IEP if you feel it does not meet your child's needs.</li>
            <li><strong>Request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</strong> If you disagree with the district's evaluation of your child, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense, as outlined in Part 200.5(g). The district must either pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to defend their evaluation.</li>
            <li><strong>Consider Mediation:</strong> If you and the school district disagree about your child's special education services, consider mediation. Mediation is a voluntary process in which a neutral third party helps you and the school district reach an agreement.</li>
            <li><strong>File a Complaint:</strong> If you believe the school district has violated your child's rights under IDEA, you can file a complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). The complaint must be filed within one year of the alleged violation.</li>
            <li><strong>Due Process Hearing:</strong> If you are unable to resolve your dispute with the school district through mediation or a complaint, you can request a due process hearing. This is a formal legal proceeding in which an impartial hearing officer will hear evidence and make a decision about your child's special education services.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="common-pitfalls-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Common District 4 Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them
    </h2>

    <div class="pitfalls-content" style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
        <p>Here are some common pitfalls that parents in District 4 may encounter and strategies to avoid them:</p>

        <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
            <li><strong>Delayed Evaluations:</strong> Districts are required to complete evaluations within 60 school days of receiving consent (Part 200.4(a)(1)). <strong>Pitfall:</strong> The district delays the evaluation process, often citing staffing shortages or scheduling conflicts. <strong>Solution:</strong> Send a certified letter requesting the evaluation, clearly stating the date. Regularly follow up with the CSE and document all communication. If the deadline is missed, file a written complaint with the NYSED.</li>
            <li><strong>Inadequate IEP Goals:</strong> <strong>Pitfall:</strong> IEP goals are vague, not measurable, or not aligned with your child's specific needs. <strong>Solution:</strong> Review the IEP carefully and request specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) goals. Provide data and observations to support your requests. If necessary, request an IEP meeting to revise the goals. Reference the NYSED IEP form and guidance documents.</li>
            <li><strong>Lack of Appropriate Services:</strong> <strong>Pitfall:</strong> The IEP does not include all the services your child needs to make progress. <strong>Solution:</strong> Advocate for specific services based on your child's evaluation results and needs. Research evidence-based interventions and present your findings to the CSE. Request a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) if behavior is impacting your child's learning. Be prepared to discuss alternative placements if the current placement is not meeting your child's needs.</li>
            <li><strong>Placement Issues:</strong> <strong>Pitfall:</strong> The district proposes a placement that is not the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE) or does not meet your child's needs. <strong>Solution:</strong> Visit potential placements and speak with teachers and administrators. Gather data on the effectiveness of different placements. Advocate for a placement that allows your child to be educated with their non-disabled peers to the maximum extent appropriate. Remember that LRE is not a place, but a principle.</li>
            <li><strong>Communication Breakdown:</strong> <strong>Pitfall:</strong> Poor communication between parents, teachers, and administrators. <strong>Solution:</strong> Establish clear communication channels with the school. Request regular progress reports and meetings. Document all communication in writing. If necessary, request a facilitator to improve communication during CSE meetings.</li>
            <li><strong>Resistance to Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs):</strong> <strong>Pitfall:</strong> The district refuses to pay for an IEE or delays the process. <strong>Solution:</strong> Know your rights regarding IEEs (Part 200.5(g)). Follow the proper procedures for requesting an IEE. If the district refuses to pay, they must initiate a due process hearing to defend their evaluation. Document all communication regarding the IEE request.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-content" style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #004085;">What is the difference between the CSE and the CPSE?</h3>
            <p>The Committee on Special Education (CSE) serves students ages 5-21. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) serves children ages 3-5. If your child is transitioning from preschool to kindergarten, their case will move from the CPSE to the CSE. In NYC the CPSE is often handled differently and can be more challenging to navigate.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #004085;">How do I request an evaluation for my child?</h3>
            <p>Send a written request to the CSE. Include your child's name, date of birth, school, and grade. Clearly state that you are requesting an evaluation for special education services. Be specific about your concerns and any areas where you believe your child is struggling. Send the request via certified mail and keep a copy for your records. The district has 60 school days to complete the evaluation process (Part 200.4(a)(1)).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #004085;">What if I disagree with the IEP?</h3>
            <p>You have the right to disagree with the IEP. Communicate your concerns to the CSE in writing. Request an IEP meeting to discuss your concerns and propose revisions to the IEP. If you are still not satisfied, you can request mediation or file a complaint with the NYSED. You also have the right to request a due process hearing.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #004085;">What is an IEE and how do I request one?</h3>
            <p>An Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) is an evaluation conducted by a qualified professional who is not employed by the school district. You have the right to request an IEE if you disagree with the district's evaluation of your child (Part 200.5(g)). To request an IEE, send a written request to the CSE. The district must either pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to defend their evaluation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #004085;">What are my options if my child is not making progress in their current placement?</h3>
            <p>If your child is not making progress, communicate your concerns to the CSE. Request an IEP meeting to review your child's progress and discuss potential changes to the IEP or placement. Consider requesting additional services, such as tutoring or specialized instruction. If necessary, explore alternative placements that may be more appropriate for your child's needs.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
    </div>
</main>
